. 
. * HYPOTHESIS FOR H3* 
. 
. *A linear OLS regression testing whether having daughters is associated with 
> a more equal division of household labor between partners, conditional on hav
> ing at least one child. Our dependent variable measures the absolute deviatio
> n from an equal split of total household and childcare hours between the resp
> ondent and their partner. It is computed as the absolute value of the respond
> ent's share of total hours minus 0.5. A value of 0 indicates perfectly equal 
> division, while higher values reflect greater inequality (regardless of which
>  partner does more). We constructed this variable using the SOEP questions pl
> i0043_h and pli0044_h, which ask about time spent on household and childcare 
> tasks on a typical weekday by the respondent and their partner, respectively.
. 
. *Outcome variable: share (defined as the absolute deviation from an equal spl
> it of total household and childcare work between respondent and partner; 0 = 
> equal sharing, higher values = greater inequality)
. 
. * Main explanatory variable: d_daughter (a dummy equal to 1 if the respondent
>  has at least one daughter, and 0 otherwise)
. 
. *We control for standard demographic covariates:
. 
. *sex (0 = female, 1 = male),
. *age,
. *years of education (pgbilzeit),
. *log of household net income (ln_hghinc),
. *number of children,
. *region (east = 1 if living in East Germany),
. *and include fixed effects for survey year (syear).
. 
. *We also control for employment status (emp_cat) and marital status (marital_
> cat) to account for socioeconomic status and life-cycle differences, as well 
> as the respondent's reported work hours (pgtatzeit).
. 
. *The model is estimated using heteroskedasticity-robust standard errors.
. 
. 
. local controls sex age pgbilzeit ln_hghinc num_children east i.emp_cat i.mari
> tal_cat i.syear pgtatzeit

. 
.         reg share d_daughter `controls', robust

